\chapter{Solver Architecture of the UKG-SE System}

\section{Introduction}
The Unified Kinematic–Geometric Simulation Engine (UKG-SE) integrates gravitational, 
geometric, and kinematic solvers into a single computational framework. This chapter 
details the architecture of the solver modules, their interactions, and the numerical 
methods used to propagate tensors, momentum flux, and curvature through dynamically 
evolving spacetime.

\section{Overview of Solver Modules}

UKG-SE consists of three primary solver modules:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Covariant Gravitational Field Solver (CGFS)}
    \item \textbf{Geometric Differential Manifold Solver (GDMS)}
    \item \textbf{Unified Kinematic Equations Solver (UKES)}
\end{enumerate}

Each module operates independently but exchanges tensor fields through a unified 
residual engine.

\section{Covariant Gravitational Field Solver (CGFS)}

\subsection{Purpose}
CGFS computes the Einstein tensor:
\begin{equation}
G_{\mu\nu} = R_{\mu\nu} - \frac{1}{2} R g_{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
from the metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ and its derivatives.

\subsection{Numerical Method}
CGFS uses a finite-difference approximation for Christoffel symbols:
\begin{equation}
\Gamma^{\rho}_{\mu\nu} \approx
\frac{1}{2} g^{\rho\sigma}
\left(
\partial_{\mu} g_{\sigma\nu}
+ \partial_{\nu} g_{\sigma\mu}
- \partial_{\sigma} g_{\mu\nu}
\right).
\end{equation}

Curvature tensors are computed using:
\begin{equation}
R^{\rho}{}_{\sigma\mu\nu} =
\partial_{\mu}\Gamma^{\rho}_{\sigma\nu}
- \partial_{\nu}\Gamma^{\rho}_{\sigma\mu}
+ \Gamma^{\rho}_{\alpha\mu}\Gamma^{\alpha}_{\sigma\nu}
- \Gamma^{\rho}_{\alpha\nu}\Gamma^{\alpha}_{\sigma\mu}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Output}
CGFS outputs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $G_{\mu\nu}$ (Einstein tensor),
    \item $R_{\mu\nu}$ (Ricci tensor),
    \item $R$ (scalar curvature),
    \item $\Gamma^{\rho}_{\mu\nu}$ (connection coefficients).
\end{itemize}

These fields feed directly into GDMS and UKES.

